# 5. Application boundaries

## 5.1 apps/headquarter

Headquarter is the frontend and human-facing command center. It replaces the generic app name web.

Responsibilities:
- RIOTWORKS visual experience.
- ARSY DECK and all product workspaces.
- Navigation, forms, charts, tables, interactions, notifications.
- Typed API client using packages/contract and packages/types.
- Locale, theme, workspace context, and user experience.
- Display of task status, approvals, financial summaries, and audit information.

Headquarter MUST NOT:
- Connect directly to PocketBase as superuser.
- contain database drivers or ORM persistence logic.
- hold API keys, marketplace secrets, or provider credentials.
- execute privileged marketplace mutations.
- calculate authoritative financial ledger state in browser code.
- bypass ARSY authorization.

Use API access through ARSY. Browser-only state is limited to interaction state and safe UI preferences.

Suggested frontend: Next.js App Router, React, TypeScript, Tailwind CSS, reusable components, and the RIOTWORKS design system.

## 5.2 apps/arsy

ARSY is the Fastify backend application and composition root.

Responsibilities:
- Build and start the Fastify server.
- Load configuration and validate environment.
- Register platform plugins and arsy-core.
- Initialize ORM, database adapter, and addons.
- Register module routes and application services.
- Compose authentication, authorization, validation, rate limits, and error handling.
- Expose health/readiness endpoints.
- Manage graceful shutdown and resource cleanup.

apps/arsy contains wiring and app-specific configuration, not duplicated framework internals or shared domain model definitions.

Use supported Fastify v5 APIs and TypeScript. Use official Fastify plugin registration and encapsulation patterns. Register infrastructure plugins before application modules/routes. Use typed predictable plugin/decorator registration.

## 5.3 packages/arsy-core

A reusable Fastify-based application platform used by apps/arsy.

Responsibilities:
- createArsyApp() / application factory.
- Plugin registration and dependency ordering.
- Typed runtime context.
- Lifecycle: configure → register plugins → initialize addons → ready → close.
- Shared configuration validation.
- Standard error mapping.
- Request correlation and structured logging.
- Health/readiness registration.
- Graceful close and resource disposal.
- Extension points for route modules and services.

Keep arsy-core independent of RIOTWORKS business entities. It may know how to load plugins and addon manifests, but it must not own Project or Intelligent domain models.

Fastify remains the HTTP framework. Do not fork or recreate Fastify.

# 6. Custom ORM — Odoo-inspired, TypeScript-native

Build RIOTWORKS' own modular ORM in packages/orm. Its design may learn from Odoo's metadata-driven models, model registry, addon manifests, dependency ordering, and model extension approach.

Do not copy Odoo source code or claim API compatibility. Implement an idiomatic TypeScript architecture.

The ORM must be metadata-driven and addon-aware.

## 6.1 Required ORM capabilities

Core:
- Model registry.
- Addon/module registry.
- Declarative model and field metadata.
- Model inheritance and extension.
- Field validation and defaults.
- Relation metadata.
- Query/domain representation.
- Safe query compilation through adapters.
- Create/read/update/archive/restore/delete policy.
- Model lifecycle hooks.
- Environment/context.
- Permission/record-scope integration points.
- Migration coordination.
- Adapter capability checking.
- Typed model APIs.
- Error normalization.

Initial field types:
- string
- text
- boolean
- integer
- float
- decimal (preserve precision; never silently treat money as binary float)
- date
- datetime
- JSON
- enum/selection
- UUID/identifier
- relation: many-to-one, one-to-many, many-to-many where adapter supports it

Query API:
- findById
- findOne
- search
- count
- create
- createMany only when supported
- update
- updateMany only when supported
- archive / restore
- delete only when policy permits
- pagination, sort, projection, and filter/domain expressions

Query operators must be validated against field metadata and adapter capability. Never build query strings by concatenating untrusted user input. Unsupported operations must fail explicitly rather than silently changing semantics.

## 6.2 Declarative model example

Implement a clean TypeScript declaration style, for example:

    export const Project = defineModel({
      name: "project.project",
      label: "Project",
      table: "projects",
      fields: {
        name: fields.string({ required: true, maxLength: 160 }),
        description: fields.text({ nullable: true }),
        state: fields.selection({
          values: ["draft", "active", "completed", "cancelled"],
          default: "draft"
        }),
        startAt: fields.datetime({ nullable: true }),
        ownerId: fields.relation.belongsTo("base.user", {
          nullable: true
        })
      },
      indexes: [
        index("project_name_idx", ["name"])
      ]
    });

This is illustrative. Design actual types and builders so invalid combinations are caught at compile time and validated at runtime.

## 6.3 Model extension

Support:
- New models.
- Extension of an existing model through addon declarations.
- Field addition with conflict checks.
- Method/service extension with deterministic ordering.
- Explicit dependency declarations.
- Stable registry composition.
- Conflict errors for duplicate field names, incompatible types, circular dependencies, and ambiguous overrides.

Do not use uncontrolled runtime monkey-patching, arbitrary eval, or arbitrary code loading from untrusted addons.

## 6.4 ORM environment

Provide a scoped execution environment containing:
- Adapter/database context.
- Workspace/tenant context where enabled.
- Actor/user identity.
- Permission context.
- Locale/timezone where relevant.
- Request/trace ID.
- Transaction capability or transaction handle when available.
- Unit-of-work scope.

Do not keep mutable per-request user context in global singletons.

