## 7.2 packages/orm-db-adaptor

Own provider-neutral adapter contracts:
- OrmDatabaseAdapter.
- AdapterCapabilities.
- Query request/result contracts.
- Schema/migration capability contracts.
- Transaction interface.
- Relation and realtime capability flags.
- Provider error mapping.
- Adapter registry and factory.

Capability declaration must include at least:
- CRUD.
- bulk create/update.
- filtering.
- pagination.
- sorting.
- projection.
- relation expansion.
- join support.
- transactions.
- schema introspection.
- schema migrations.
- unique constraints.
- indexes.
- realtime events.
- soft delete.
- optimistic concurrency.
- JSON operators.

The ORM must ask the adapter before using any non-basic operation.

## 7.3 packages/orm-db-adaptor-pocketbase

Create the first concrete PocketBase adapter using the official PocketBase JavaScript SDK or documented Web API from the server side.

Architecture:
- PocketBase is a separate Go-based service, not a Node database driver embedded inside ARSY.
- ARSY communicates with PocketBase through the adapter.
- The adapter owns SDK/client setup, server-side authentication, collection mapping, filter compiler, record mapping, and provider errors.
- Superuser credentials/tokens remain server-side, protected by environment/secret management.
- Headquarter never receives a superuser credential or bypasses ARSY.
- Do not connect directly to PocketBase from browser code for privileged data.

Important limitations:
- PocketBase collections are backed by SQLite tables and expose PocketBase-specific record/collection APIs.
- It is not a general SQL adapter and must not pretend to support arbitrary SQL joins.
- Relation expansion and filtering must be limited to documented PocketBase capabilities.
- Transaction support must be reported as unavailable through the ordinary remote SDK adapter unless a separately verified official mechanism is implemented.
- Schema changes should use PocketBase's supported migration/deployment mechanism. Do not mutate internal SQLite tables directly.
- View collections are read-only; the adapter must respect that.
- Auth collection special fields and rules must not be treated as ordinary base-model fields without explicit mapping.
- Realtime is optional and must be explicitly capability-gated.
- If an ORM feature cannot be implemented correctly on PocketBase, reject it with a typed UnsupportedCapabilityError; never silently emulate unsafe semantics.

Create a compatibility matrix and adapter tests. Do not state that PocketBase provides full Odoo ORM capabilities.

## 7.4 Future database adapters

Keep adapter interfaces open for a future PostgreSQL adapter and other stores. Do not add them unless needed. The domain ORM must not import PocketBase SDK types.

# 8. Addon system — Odoo-inspired module architecture

The ORM addon system is a first-class architectural capability.

An addon is a versioned, trusted code package that declares:
- name and technical key.
- version.
- description.
- dependencies.
- compatible platform/ORM versions.
- models it creates.
- models it extends.
- services and hooks.
- migrations.
- permissions/capabilities.
- routes or application integration declarations where appropriate.
- install, upgrade, and uninstall behavior.

Example manifest:

    export default defineAddon({
      name: "project",
      version: "1.0.0",
      depends: ["base"],
      models: [Project, ProjectTask],
      migrations: projectMigrations
    });

Required lifecycle:
DISCOVER → VALIDATE → RESOLVE DEPENDENCIES → REGISTER METADATA → APPLY MIGRATIONS → INITIALIZE SERVICES → READY.

Rules:
- Resolve dependencies deterministically.
- Reject missing dependencies and circular dependency graphs.
- Reject duplicate addon IDs and model conflicts.
- Respect semantic version compatibility.
- Do not execute migrations before dependency validation.
- Do not silently remove data when uninstalling.
- Use explicit migration/upgrade plans.
- Keep an addon registry snapshot and installed version.
- Addons must not reach into another addon's private files.
- Public exports and extension contracts are the only supported integration boundary.
- Only trusted installed code may execute. Never install or execute arbitrary community addon code automatically.

# 9. Base addon — packages/orm-addon-base

This addon is foundational and must be installed automatically before all business addons.

It defines the shared abstract base entity and mixins so every addon does not need to repeat common fields and audit implementation.

## 9.1 BaseEntity

Every persisted business model should receive base metadata automatically through ORM model composition or a single inherited abstract base.

Required standard fields:
- id: generated stable identifier.
- active: boolean, default true; the canonical active/inactive lifecycle flag.
- createdAt: creation timestamp.
- updatedAt: last modification timestamp.
- createdBy: actor/user reference when available.
- updatedBy: actor/user reference when available.
- version: optional optimistic concurrency version when supported.

Optional centrally managed fields:
- archivedAt.
- archivedBy.

Do not define these repeatedly in Project, Intelligent, or future addons.

The active flag is not the same as a domain-specific workflow status. A Project may have state = completed while remaining active = true. Deactivation/archiving is a separate lifecycle concern.

Default search behavior for ordinary business listings should exclude inactive records unless explicitly requested. Provide includeInactive or equivalent context/query option. Do not hard-delete a record merely because active becomes false.

## 9.2 AuditTrail

Implement one shared audit subsystem in the base addon and ORM lifecycle.

Central audit behavior covers:
- create.
- update.
- archive/deactivate.
- restore/reactivate.
- delete where explicitly allowed.
- sensitive administrative changes.
- addon/schema changes where appropriate.

Audit event fields:
- audit ID.
- model technical name.
- record ID.
- action.
- actor ID/type.
- timestamp.
- request/trace ID.
- source (API, system, agent, import, migration).
- changed field names.
- before/after diff where safe.
- metadata and reason where applicable.

Rules:
- Audit events are append-only through normal application flows.
- Redact secrets and sensitive personal data.
- Do not store raw credentials or prompt secrets in diffs.
- Audit history must be permission-protected.
- Audit trail is centrally generated from ORM lifecycle operations; individual addons must not reimplement it.
- Use a transaction when the adapter supports it.
- When an adapter cannot guarantee atomic writes, declare the limitation and use a durable outbox/reconciliation pattern or fail closed for operations that require guaranteed auditing.
- Never claim tamper-proof storage unless a real integrity mechanism is implemented and verified.

## 9.3 Other base services

The base addon may provide shared abstractions for common name/description mixins, workspace ownership fields when enabled, active/archive service, actor context, audit service, timestamps, common constraints, and shared access-control metadata.

Do not force every model to have irrelevant business fields. Only universally applicable system fields belong on BaseEntity.

